    "questionText": "A 38-year-old male with a history of poorly controlled HIV (CD4 count 45 cells/mm^3) arrives at the clinic with a 2-week history of gradually worsening headache, nausea, and low-grade fevers. He initially attributed his symptoms to a cold, but the headache has become relentless. Neurologic examination reveals mild nuchal rigidity. An MRI of the brain is unremarkable. A lumbar puncture reveals an opening pressure of 280 mm H2O. CSF analysis shows a predominant lymphocytosis, elevated protein, and low glucose. A specialized stain of the CSF highlights yeast cells surrounded by a thick, clear halo. Which of the following best describes the primary virulence factor responsible for the stained appearance of this organism?",
